<article class="env-lemma" id="0ALV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ALV">Lemma <span data-tag="0ALV">10.61.2</span>.</a> A Noetherian ring with finitely many primes has dimension $\leq 1$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R$ be a Noetherian ring with finitely many primes. If $R$ is a local domain, then the lemma follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/02IG" data-tag="02IG">10.61.1</a>. If $R$ is a domain, then $R_\mathfrak m$ has dimension $\leq 1$ for all maximal ideals $\mathfrak m$ by the local case. Hence $\dim (R) \leq 1$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/00KG" data-tag="00KG">10.60.4</a>. If $R$ is general, then $\dim (R/\mathfrak q) \leq 1$ for every minimal prime $\mathfrak q$ of $R$. Since every prime contains a minimal prime (Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a>), this implies $\dim (R) \leq 1$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>